It comes from the device's MAC address, not from a photo. The first three pairs of a MAC (the OUI) are a block the IEEE assigns to one company, so RackTrack can name who built a device it has never seen — that is how a MAC hanging off a port gets labelled Dell, Realtek, Apple and so on. RackTrack keeps a copy of the IEEE registry on the server and the app asks it only for the prefixes it is about to draw. Note this is the maker of the network chip, not necessarily the brand on the box.

If only some rows say "Unknown maker" while others are named, the lookup is working and one of these is true:
- the block is registered as "Private" — the owner paid the IEEE to hide the name, so there is nothing to show (RackTrack deliberately stores no name for these);
- the block is a delegated/shared IEEE range with no single owner;
- the block is newer than RackTrack's copy of the registry, which is refreshed periodically rather than live;
- the address is locally administered or randomised (common on phones and some virtual machines), so it belongs to no manufacturer at all.

None of those are fixable from the field, and none affect port state, link speed or cabling — the MAC itself is still correct.

The one case worth reporting: if every device on the port says "Unknown maker", the vendor lookup itself failed (server unreachable, session expired, or the vendor table missing on the server) rather than the prefixes being unknown. Reloading the app clears the cached result and retries; if it still shows all-Unknown, raise it with your admin.

Either way, treat the vendor name as a hint for identifying what is plugged in and confirm from the device itself before acting on it.

